{
  "assessment": "The paper presents a microscopic theory of nuclear spin cooling and heating by strongly localized electrons, with explicit equations for the inverse nuclear spin temperature and the electron-induced nuclear spin-lattice relaxation rate. The main quantitative cluster is the dependence of the inverse nuclear spin temperature on external magnetic field and electron correlation time, with two regimes (short and long correlation time) that share the same controls and readout. The source provides explicit formulas, parameter values (e.g., Phi=3/2 and 1/2), and a fitted expression for the optimal field. The extraction is case_authoring_ready because it supports a numeric control (magnetic field), a numeric readout (inverse nuclear spin temperature), a quantitative mechanism, and an objective response within an explicit domain.",
  "case_authoring_ready": true,
  "source": {
    "citation": "D. S. Smirnov, Cooling and Heating Nuclear Spins by Strongly Localized Electrons, Physical Review Letters (2025), DOI:10.1103/physrevlett.134.016201",
    "journal_doi": "10.1103/physrevlett.134.016201",
    "arxiv_versioned_id": "2406.08296v2"
  },
  "paper_scope": "The paper develops a microscopic theory of dynamic nuclear spin polarization and relaxation by strongly localized electrons in quantum dots, bridging the spin-temperature approach and the central spin model. It derives expressions for the inverse nuclear spin temperature under optical pumping and for the electron-induced nuclear spin-lattice relaxation rate, and analyzes their dependence on external magnetic field and electron correlation time.",
  "mechanisms": [
    {
      "mechanism_id": "inverse_spin_temp_mechanism",
      "statement": "The inverse nuclear spin temperature beta established under dynamic polarization by electrons is given by Eq. (eq:beta): beta = 4<S_z>B/(hbar*gamma_N) / [B^2 + Phi*(T_{1,e}^N/tau_c)*(tau_c/T_{2,e}^*)^2*B_L^2], where T_{2,e}^* = hbar/(a*sqrt(N_I)) with N_I = 4NI(I+1)/3. This is derived from the balance of energy fluxes (Eq. eq:balance) and the expressions for q_Z and q_SS.",
      "equations": [
        "beta = 4<S_z>B/(hbar*gamma_N) / [B^2 + Phi*(T_{1,e}^N/tau_c)*(tau_c/T_{2,e}^*)^2*B_L^2]",
        "T_{2,e}^* = hbar/(a*sqrt(N_I))",
        "N_I = 4NI(I+1)/3"
      ],
      "variable_names": [
        "beta",
        "B",
        "S_z",
        "gamma_N",
        "Phi",
        "T_{1,e}^N",
        "tau_c",
        "T_{2,e}^*",
        "B_L",
        "a",
        "N_I",
        "N",
        "I"
      ],
      "parameters": [
        {
          "name": "Phi_short",
          "value": 1.5,
          "uncertainty": "none",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "coefficient in the short-correlation-time limit",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 134,
              "line_end": 134,
              "support": "In the limit of short correlation time, Eq. (eq:T_eN) reduces to T_{1,e}^N=1/(<Omega_{e,x}^2+Omega_{e,y}^2>tau_c), so Eq. (eq:beta) reproduces the classical result of the Dyakonov-Perel theory, to which end one should put Phi=3/2."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "Phi_long",
          "value": 0.5,
          "uncertainty": "none",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "coefficient in the long-correlation-time limit",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 136,
              "line_end": 136,
              "support": "In the opposite limit of long correlation time, Phi then becomes equal to 1/2."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 127,
          "line_end": 132,
          "support": "Substituting Eqs. (eq:q_Z) and (eq:q_SS) in Eq. (eq:balance) and making use of Eq. (eq:P_N) we obtain the inverse spin temperature established under dynamic polarization by electrons: beta = 4<S_z>B/(hbar*gamma_N) / [B^2 + Phi*(T_{1,e}^N/tau_c)*(tau_c/T_{2,e}^*)^2*B_L^2], where we have set T_{2,e}^* = hbar/(a*sqrt(N_I)) with N_I = 4NI(I+1)/3."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "mechanism_id": "heating_rate_mechanism",
      "statement": "The electron-induced nuclear spin-lattice relaxation rate is given by Eq. (eq:TE): 1/T_E = -dot(beta)/beta = f/[N_I*(B^2+B_L^2)] * (B^2/T_{1,e}^N + Phi*tau_c*B_L^2/T_{2,e}^{*2}). This is derived from the dynamics of the inverse spin temperature in the dark, with the heat capacity of the NSS given by Eq. (eq:C).",
      "equations": [
        "1/T_E = f/[N_I*(B^2+B_L^2)] * (B^2/T_{1,e}^N + Phi*tau_c*B_L^2/T_{2,e}^{*2})",
        "-dE_NSS/dbeta = N_I*(hbar*gamma_N/2)^2*(B^2+B_L^2)"
      ],
      "variable_names": [
        "T_E",
        "beta",
        "f",
        "N_I",
        "B",
        "B_L",
        "T_{1,e}^N",
        "Phi",
        "tau_c",
        "T_{2,e}^*",
        "gamma_N"
      ],
      "parameters": [],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 168,
          "line_end": 180,
          "support": "In the dark, the dynamics of the inverse spin temperature is determined by warming up of Zeeman and spin-spin energy reservoirs: dot(beta) = (q_Z+q_SS)/(dE_NSS/dbeta), where -dE_NSS/dbeta = N_I*(hbar*gamma_N/2)^2*(B^2+B_L^2) is the heat capacity of the NSS. This leads to the general expression for the electron-induced nuclear spin-lattice relaxation rate: 1/T_E = -dot(beta)/beta = f/[N_I*(B^2+B_L^2)] * (B^2/T_{1,e}^N + Phi*tau_c*B_L^2/T_{2,e}^{*2})."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
